examples.question_text:
Describe three sequences that transform $y=f(x)$ into $y=f(8-2x)$, beginning with:
(a) a translation;
(b) a horizontal dilation;
(c) a reflection in the $y$-axis.

examples.solution_text:
(a) Translating gives $y=(x-6)^2$. Replacing $x$ with $3x$ then gives $y=(3x-6)^2=9(x-2)^2$.
The vertex moves from $(0,0)$ to $(6,0)$, then to $(2,0)$.

(b) Dilating gives $y=(3x)^2$. Replacing $x$ with $x-6$ then gives $y=9(x-6)^2$.
The vertex stays at $(0,0)$ during dilation, then moves to $(6,0)$.

Both curves have the same shape, but their vertices differ because the order changes the final horizontal displacement.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=3mm,>=stealth]
\definecolor{curveA}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{curveB}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-0.5,0)--(8,0) node[right,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-1)--(0,11) node[above,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$y$};
\draw[curveA,thick,<->,domain=1:3,samples=60] plot (\x,{9*(\x-2)*(\x-2)});
\draw[curveB,thick,<->,domain=5:7,samples=60] plot (\x,{9*(\x-6)*(\x-6)});
\fill[curveA] (2,0) circle (1.2pt);
\fill[curveB] (6,0) circle (1.2pt);
\path (2,-0.2) node[below,black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$(2,0)$};
\path (6,-0.2) node[below,black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {$(6,0)$};
\path (2,10) node[curveA,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {(a)};
\path (6,10) node[curveB,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] {(b)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
